11. Insert the sum of each pair of adjacent terms between them in a sequence, forming a new sequence, which is called a "Z-extension" of the sequence. Given the sequence $1,2,3$, after the first "Z-extension" it becomes $1,3,2,5,3$; after the second "Z-extension" it becomes $1,4,3,5,2,7,5,8,3 ; \cdots$; after the $n$-th "Z-extension" it becomes $1, x_{1}, x_{2}, \cdots, x_{m}, 3$, and let $a_{n}=1+x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{m}+3$.
(1) Find the values of $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}$;
(2) If $b_{n}=a_{n}-2$, prove that the sequence $\left\{b_{n}\right\}$ is a geometric sequence, and find the general term formula for the sequence $\left\{a_{n}\right\}$.

9. (12 points) Five students, Jia, Yi, Bing, Ding, and Wu, ranked in the top 5 (no ties) in a math competition and stood in a row for a photo. They each said the following:

Jia said: The two students next to me have ranks that are both behind mine;
Yi said: The two students next to me have ranks that are adjacent to mine;
Bing said: All the students to my right (at least 1) have ranks that are ahead of mine;
Ding said: All the students to my left (at least 1) have ranks that are behind mine;
Wu said: I am standing in the second position from the right.

Given that they are all honest children, Jia, Yi, Bing, Ding, and Wu received ranks $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ respectively. What is the five-digit number $\overline{\mathrm{ABCDE}}$? $\qquad$

Given a set of points on a plane $P=\left\{P_{1}, P_{2}, \cdots, P_{1994}\right\}$, where no three points in $P$ are collinear, divide all the points in $P$ into 83 groups arbitrarily, such that each group has at least 3 points, and each point belongs to exactly one group. Then, connect any two points in the same group with a line segment, and do not connect points in different groups, thus obtaining a pattern $G$. Different grouping methods result in different patterns. Let the number of triangles in the pattern $G$ with vertices from the points in $P$ be denoted as $m(G)$.

(1) Find the minimum value $m_{0}$ of $m(G)$;
(2) Let $G^{*}$ be a pattern such that $m\left(G^{*}\right)=m_{0}$. If the line segments in $G^{*}$ (referring to line segments with endpoints from the points in $P$) are colored using 4 colors, with each line segment being colored exactly one color, prove that there exists a coloring scheme such that $G^{*}$, after coloring, does not contain a triangle with all three sides of the same color.

Example 2 Let $\left\{a_{n}\right\}$ be the sequence of all numbers in the set $\left\{2^{s}+2^{t} \mid 10 \leqslant s<t\right.$ and $s, t \in \mathbf{Z}\}$ arranged in ascending order, i.e., $\square$
$$
\begin{aligned}
& a_{1}=3, a_{2}=5, a_{3}=6, a_{4}=9, a_{5}=10, \\
a_{6}= & 12, \cdots .
\end{aligned}
$$

Arrange the terms of the sequence $\left\{a_{n}\right\}$ in a triangular number table according to the principle of smaller on top, larger on the bottom, and smaller on the left, larger on the right:
$$
\begin{array}{l}
3 \\
5 \quad 6 \\
\begin{array}{lll}
9 & 10 \quad 12
\end{array} \\
\text {... ... ... ... } \\
\end{array}
$$
(1) Write out the numbers in the fourth and fifth rows of this triangular number table;
(2) $a_{100}$.

\section*{Task 1 - 121011}

Draw in oblique parallel projection four plane-faced bodies, each with exactly 6 vertices, where the first has exactly 5, the second exactly 6, the third exactly 7, and the fourth exactly 8 faces!

Determine the number of all edges for each of these bodies!

5. (10 points) There are four cards, each with the numbers $2$, $0$, $1$, and $5$ written on them. Jia, Yi, Bing, and Ding each received one card.
Jia says: The numbers you three have do not include a number that is 1 less than the number I have;
Yi says: The numbers you three have must include a number that is 1 less than the number I have;
Bing says: The number I have cannot be the first digit of a four-digit number;
Ding says: The number I have cannot be the last digit of a four-digit number. If it is found that those who have even numbers always lie, while those who have odd numbers always tell the truth. Then the four-digit number formed by the numbers Jia, Yi, Bing, and Ding have in sequence is $\qquad$

Three. (20 points) Given a sequence $\left\{a_{n}\right\}$ with all terms no less than 1, satisfying: $a_{1}=1, a_{2}=1+\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\left(\frac{a_{n}}{a_{n+1}-1}\right)^{2}+$ $\left(\frac{a_{n}-1}{a_{n-1}}\right)^{2}=2$. Try to find:
(1) The general term formula of the sequence $\left\{a_{n}\right\}$;
(2) The value of $\lim _{n \rightarrow+\infty} \frac{a_{n}}{n}$.

\section*{Problem 4 - 151044}

Determine all unordered pairs \((x, y)\) of two natural numbers \(x, y\) with \(x \neq y\), for which the following holds!

The arithmetic mean of \(x\) and \(y\) is a two-digit number. If one swaps the digits of this number, one obtains the geometric mean of \(x\) and \(y\) (which is the number \(\sqrt{x y}\)).

## Aufgabe 1 - 281241

Man ermittle alle reellen Lösungen $(x, y, z)$ des Gleichungssystems

$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2} & =1 \\
x+2 y+3 z & =\sqrt{14}
\end{aligned}
$$

Three, (20 points) Find all functions $f: \mathbf{R} \rightarrow \mathbf{R}$ such that for any $x, y \in \mathbf{R}$, we have
$$
\begin{array}{l}
f(4 x-y-1)+4 f(x+y)-16 f(x) \\
=(6 x-2)^{2}+45 y^{2}
\end{array}
$$

Problem 7.7.1. A seven-digit natural number $N$ is called interesting if:

- it consists of non-zero digits;
- it is divisible by 4;
- any number obtained from the number $N$ by permuting its digits is divisible by 4.

How many interesting numbers exist?

1. A model car is tested on some closed circuits that are 600 meters long, consisting of flat sections and uphill or downhill sections. All the uphill and downhill sections have the same gradient. The tests highlight some curious facts:

(a) the speed of the model car depends only on whether the car is on an uphill, flat, or downhill section; calling these three speeds $v_{s}, v_{p}$, and $v_{d}$, respectively, we have $v_{s}<v_{p}<v_{d}$;

(b) $v_{s}, v_{p}$, and $v_{d}$, expressed in meters per second, are integers;

(c) regardless of the composition of the circuit (with more or fewer uphill and downhill sections), the travel time is always 50 seconds.

Find all possible values of $v_{s}, v_{p}$, and $v_{d}$.

9 . Arrange the odd positive integers $1,3,5,7, \cdots$ in five columns, as shown in the table below. The leftmost column is called the 1st column, and the columns are numbered from left to right. Thus, the number “1985” appears in
(A) the 1st column;
(B) the 2nd column;
(C) the 3rd column;
(D) the 4th column;
(E) the 5th column.
\begin{tabular}{|ccccc|}
\hline & 1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\
15 & 13 & 11 & 9 & \\
& 17 & 19 & 21 & 23 \\
31 & 29 & 27 & 25 & \\
& 33 & 35 & 37 & 39 \\
47 & 15 & 43 & 41 & \\
& 19 & 51 & 53 & 55 \\
$\cdot$ & $\cdot$ & $\cdot$ & $\cdot$ & \\
& $\cdot$ & $\cdot$ & $\cdot$ & $\cdot$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

2. The diagonal of a square is $2 \sqrt{2}$, and the two diagonals coincide with the coordinate axes. Then the distance from the origin to one side of the square is:
(A) 1;
(B) $\backslash 2$;
(C) 2 ;
(D) $2 \sqrt{2}$.

\section*{Problem 5 - 331035}

Determine all pairs \((m ; n)\) of positive integers \(m\) and \(n\) for which

\[
\frac{m^{2}}{m+1}+\frac{n^{2}}{n+1}
\]

is an integer.

Points $A, B,$ and $C$ are on a circle such that $\triangle ABC$ is an acute triangle. $X, Y ,$ and $Z$ are on the circle such that $AX$ is perpendicular to $BC$ at $D$, $BY$ is perpendicular to $AC$ at $E$, and $CZ$ is perpendicular to $AB$ at $F$. Find the value of \[ \frac{AX}{AD}+\frac{BY}{BE}+\frac{CZ}{CF},  \] and prove that this value is the same for all possible $A, B, C$ on the circle such that $\triangle ABC$ is acute.

[asy]
pathpen = linewidth(0.7);
pair B = (0,0), C = (10,0), A = (2.5,8); path cir = circumcircle(A,B,C);
pair D = foot(A,B,C), E = foot(B,A,C), F = foot(C,A,B), X = IP(D--2*D-A,cir), Y = IP(E--2*E-B,cir), Z = IP(F--2*F-C,cir); D(MP("A",A,N)--MP("B",B,SW)--MP("C",C,SE)--cycle); D(cir); D(A--MP("X",X)); D(B--MP("Y",Y,NE)); D(C--MP("Z",Z,NW)); D(rightanglemark(B,F,C,12)); D(rightanglemark(A,D,B,12)); D(rightanglemark(B,E,C,12));[/asy]

9. (16 points) $P$ is a moving point on the ellipse $\frac{x^{2}}{4}+\frac{y^{2}}{3}=1$. A tangent line is drawn through point $P$ to the ellipse, intersecting the circle $\odot O$ : $x^{2}+y^{2}=12$ at points $M$ and $N$. The tangents to $\odot O$ at points $M$ and $N$ intersect at point $Q$.
(1) Find the equation of the locus of point $Q$;
(2) If $P$ is a point in the first quadrant, find the maximum value of the area of $\triangle O P Q$.

## Problem Statement

Calculate the lengths of the arcs of the curves given by the parametric equations.

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left\{\begin{array}{l}
x=e^{t}(\cos t+\sin t) \\
y=e^{t}(\cos t-\sin t)
\end{array}\right. \\
& 0 \leq t \leq \frac{3 \pi}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

8. If one edge length of a tetrahedron is $x$, and the other edges are all 1, the volume is $F(x)$, then the function $F(x)$ on its domain
(A) is an increasing function but has no maximum value;
(B) is an increasing function and has a maximum value;
(C) is not an increasing function and has no maximum value;
(D) is not an increasing function but has a maximum value.
Answer ( )

Given positive numbers $x, y, z$ satisfying $x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=1$.
(1) Find the minimum value of $\frac{x^{2} y}{z^{2}}+\frac{y^{2} z}{x^{2}}+\frac{z^{2} x}{y^{2}}$;
(2) Find the minimum value of $\frac{x^{2} y^{3}}{z^{4}}+\frac{y^{2} z^{3}}{x^{4}}+\frac{z^{2} x^{3}}{y^{4}}$.

14. As shown in Figure 7, $\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles right triangle, $\angle C = 90^{\circ}, O$ is a point inside $\triangle ABC$, and the distances from point $O$ to the sides of $\triangle ABC$ are all equal to 1. $\triangle ABC$ is rotated $45^{\circ}$ clockwise around point $O$ to get $\triangle A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}$, and the common part of the two triangles is the polygon $KLMNPQ$.
(1) Prove: $\triangle AKL, \triangle BMN, \triangle CPQ$ are all isosceles right triangles;
(2) Find the area of the common part of $\triangle ABC$ and $\triangle A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}$.

15) As shown in the figure, the center of the hyperbola is at the origin $O$, and the foci are on the $x$-axis. The two asymptotes are $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$. A line passing through the right focus $F$ and perpendicular to $l_{1}$ intersects $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. It is also known that the eccentricity of the hyperbola $e=\frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}$.
(1) Prove that $|\overrightarrow{O A}|$, $|\overrightarrow{A B}|$, and $|\overrightarrow{O B}|$ form an arithmetic sequence;
(2) If $F(\sqrt{5}, 0)$, find the length of the chord $C D$ intercepted by the line $A B$ on the hyperbola.

3. In the $x y$-plane, which of the following is the reflection of the graph of
$$
y=\frac{1+x}{1+x^{2}}
$$
about the line $y=2 x$ ?
(A) $x=\frac{1+y}{1+y^{2}}$;
(B) $x=\frac{-1+y}{1+y^{2}}$;
(C) $x=-\frac{1+y}{1+y^{2}}$
(D) $x=\frac{1-y}{1+y^{2}}$;
(E) None of the above.

The diagram below shows nine points on a circle where $AB=BC=CD=DE=EF=FG=GH$.  Given that $\angle GHJ=117^\circ$ and $\overline{BH}$ is perpendicular to $\overline{EJ}$, there are relatively prime positive integers $m$ and $n$ so that the degree measure of $\angle AJB$ is $\textstyle\frac mn$.  Find $m+n$.

[asy]
size(175);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.6));
draw(unitcircle,linewidth(0.9));
string labels[] = {"A","B","C","D","E","F","G"};
int start=110,increment=20;
pair J=dir(210),x[],H=dir(start-7*increment);
for(int i=0;i<=6;i=i+1)
{
x[i]=dir(start-increment*i);
draw(J--x[i]--H);
dot(x[i]);
label("$"+labels[i]+"$",x[i],dir(origin--x[i]));
}
draw(J--H);
dot(H^^J);
label("$H$",H,dir(origin--H));
label("$J$",J,dir(origin--J));
[/asy]

Square $ABCD$ has side length $13$, and points $E$ and $F$ are exterior to the square such that $BE=DF=5$ and $AE=CF=12$. Find $EF^{2}$.
[asy]
size(200);
defaultpen(fontsize(10));
real x=22.61986495;
pair A=(0,26), B=(26,26), C=(26,0), D=origin, E=A+24*dir(x), F=C+24*dir(180+x);
draw(B--C--F--D--C^^D--A--E--B--A, linewidth(0.7));
dot(A^^B^^C^^D^^E^^F);
pair point=(13,13);
label("$A$", A, dir(point--A));
label("$B$", B, dir(point--B));
label("$C$", C, dir(point--C));
label("$D$", D, dir(point--D));
label("$E$", E, dir(point--E));
label("$F$", F, dir(point--F));[/asy]

5 Let real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \cdots, a_{100}$ satisfy:
(1) $a_{1} \geqslant a_{2} \geqslant \cdots \geqslant a_{100} \geqslant 0$;
(2) $a_{1}+a_{2} \leqslant 100$;
(3) $a_{3}+a_{4}+\cdots+a_{100} \leqslant 100$.

Find the maximum value of $a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\cdots+a_{100}^{2}$.

5. $[x]$ represents taking the integer part of the number $x$, for example $\left[\frac{15}{4}\right]=3$, etc. If $y=4\left(\frac{x+[u]}{4}-\left[\frac{x+[u]}{4}\right]\right)$, and when
$x=1,8,11,14$, $y=1$;
$x=2,5,12,15$, $y=2$;
$x=3,6,9,16$, $y=3$;
$x=4,7,10,13$, $y=0$.
Then the expression for $u$ is
(A) $\frac{x+2}{4}$;
(B) $\frac{x+1}{4}$;
(C) $\frac{x}{4}$;
(D) $\frac{x-1}{4}$.

Three, (25 points) Divide $1,2, \cdots, 9$ into three groups, each containing three numbers, such that the sum of the numbers in each group is a prime number.
(1) Prove that there must be two groups with equal sums;
(2) Find the number of all different ways to divide them.

3. Give all possible triples $(a, b, c)$ of positive integers with the following properties:

- $\operatorname{gcd}(a, b)=\operatorname{gcd}(a, c)=\operatorname{gcd}(b, c)=1$;
- $a$ is a divisor of $a+b+c$;
- $b$ is a divisor of $a+b+c$;
- $c$ is a divisor of $a+b+c$.

(For given numbers $x$ and $y$, $\operatorname{gcd}(x, y)$ denotes the greatest common divisor of $x$ and $y$.)

2. In the plane, there are $h+s$ lines, among which $h$ lines are horizontal, and $s$ lines satisfy:
(1) None of them are horizontal lines;
(2) No two of them are parallel;
(3) Any 3 of these $h+s$ lines do not intersect at the same point.
If these $h+s$ lines exactly divide the plane into 1992 regions, find all pairs of positive integers $(h, s)$.

12. If:
(1) $a, b, c, d$ all belong to $\{1,2,3,4\}$;
(2) $a \neq b, b \neq c, c \neq d, d \neq a$;
(3) $a$ is the smallest value among $a, b, c, d$.
Then, the number of different four-digit numbers $\overline{a b c d}$ that can be formed is $\qquad$.

1. Let $p, q$ be natural numbers, condition A: $p^{3}-q^{3}$ is even; condition B: $p+q$ is even, then,
(A) A is a sufficient but not necessary condition for B;
(B) A is a necessary but not sufficient condition for B;
(C) A is a necessary and sufficient condition for B;
(D) A is neither a sufficient condition nor a necessary condition for B.

\section*{Problem 3 - 340923}

Starting from a square \(A B C D\), for any two positive integers \(x\) and \(y\), the following constructions can be performed:

The side \(A B\) is extended beyond \(B\) by a length of \(x \cdot A B\) to the point \(S\), the side \(B C\) is extended beyond \(C\) by a length of \(y \cdot B C\) to the point \(T\), the side \(C D\) is extended beyond \(D\) by a length of \(x \cdot C D\) to the point \(U\), and the side \(D A\) is extended beyond \(A\) by a length of \(y \cdot D A\) to the point \(V\). Determine all pairs \((x ; y)\) of positive integers for which the resulting quadrilateral \(S T U V\) has exactly 11 times the area of the square \(A B C D\)!

22. From $\{1,2,3, \cdots, 10\}$, six different integers are randomly selected. Among these selections, the probability that the second smallest number is 3 is
(A) $\frac{1}{60}$;
(B) $\frac{1}{6}$;
(C) $\frac{1}{3}$;
(D) $\frac{1}{2}$;
(E) none of the above.

3. (1997 National High School Competition Question) Let the two branches of the hyperbola $xy=1$ be $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$, and the three vertices of the equilateral triangle $PQR$ lie on this hyperbola.
(1) Prove that $P$, $Q$, and $R$ cannot all lie on the same branch of the hyperbola;
(2) Suppose $P(-1,-1)$ is on $C_{2}$, and $Q$, $R$ are on $C_{1}$, find the coordinates of $Q$ and $R$.

G63 (34-2, UK) Let $D$ be a point inside an acute triangle $ABC$ such that $\angle ADB = \angle ACB + 90^\circ$ and $AC \cdot BD = AD \cdot BC$.
(a) Calculate the ratio $\frac{AB \cdot CD}{AC \cdot BD}$;
(b) Prove that the tangents to the circumcircles of $\triangle ACD$ and $\triangle BCD$ at $C$ are perpendicular to each other.

(11) Let $P$ be a moving point on the ellipse $\frac{x^{2}}{4}+\frac{y^{2}}{3}=1$. Draw the tangent line to the ellipse at point $P$ and let it intersect the circle $\odot O$ : $x^{2}+y^{2}=12$ at points $M$ and $N$. The tangents to $\odot O$ at points $M$ and $N$ intersect at point $Q$.
(1) Find the equation of the locus of point $Q$;
(2) If $P$ is a point in the first quadrant, find the maximum value of the area of $\triangle O P Q$.

Find all surjective functions $f:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ such that for all positive integers $a$ and $b$, exactly one of the following equations is true:
\begin{align*}
f(a)&=f(b), <br /> \\
 f(a+b)&=\min\{f(a),f(b)\}.
\end{align*}
[i]Remarks:[/i] $\mathbb{N}$ denotes the set of all positive integers. A function $f:X\to Y$ is said to be surjective if for every $y\in Y$ there exists $x\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$.

5. In the coordinate plane, a point whose both coordinates are integers is called an integer point. We use $I$ to denote the set of all lines, $M$ to denote the set of lines passing through exactly one integer point, $N$ to denote the set of lines not passing through any integer point, and $P$ to denote the set of lines passing through infinitely many integer points. Then the number of correct statements among the following expressions:
(1) $M \cup N \bigcup P=I$;
(2) $M \neq \varnothing$;
(3) $N \neq \varnothing$;
(4) $P \neq \varnothing$
is
A. 1
B. 2
C. 3
D. 4

Let the two branches of the hyperbola $xy=1$ be $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$ (as shown in the figure), and the three vertices of the equilateral triangle $PQR$ lie on this hyperbola.
(1) Prove that $P, Q, R$ cannot all lie on the same branch of the hyperbola;
(2) Suppose $P(-1,-1)$ is on $C_{2}$, and $Q, R$ are on $C_{1}$. Find: the coordinates of the vertices $Q, R$.

The diagram below shows a circle with center $F$. The angles are related with $\angle BFC = 2\angle AFB$, $\angle CFD = 3\angle AFB$, $\angle DFE = 4\angle AFB$, and $\angle EFA = 5\angle AFB$. Find the degree measure of $\angle BFC$.
[asy]
size(4cm);
pen dps = fontsize(10);
defaultpen(dps);
dotfactor=4;
draw(unitcircle);
pair A,B,C,D,E,F;
A=dir(90);
B=dir(66);
C=dir(18);
D=dir(282);
E=dir(210);
F=origin;
dot("$F$",F,NW);
dot("$A$",A,dir(90));
dot("$B$",B,dir(66));
dot("$C$",C,dir(18));
dot("$D$",D,dir(306));
dot("$E$",E,dir(210));
draw(F--E^^F--D^^F--C^^F--B^^F--A);
[/asy]

5. Let the sequence $\left\{a_{n}\right\}$ satisfy
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\left(2-a_{n+1}\right)\left(4+a_{n}\right)=8(n \geqslant 1), \text { and } a_{1}=2 . \\
\text { Then } \frac{1}{a_{1}}+\frac{1}{a_{2}}+\cdots+\frac{1}{a_{n}}=\text {. }
\end{array}
$$

5. The size relationship of $\log _{\sin 1} \cos 17 \log _{\sin 1} \operatorname{tg} 1, \log _{\cos 1} \sin 1 \log _{\cos 1} \operatorname{tg} 1$ is
(A) $\log _{\sin 1} \cos 1<\log _{\cos 1} \sin 1<\log _{\sin 1} \operatorname{tg} 1<\log _{\cos 1} \operatorname{tg} 1$;
(B) $\log _{\cos 1} \sin 1<\log _{\cos 1} \operatorname{tg} 1<\log _{\sin 1} \cos 1<\log _{\sin 1} \operatorname{tg} 1$;
(C) $\log _{\sin 1} \operatorname{tg} 1<\log _{\cos 1} \operatorname{tg} 1<\log _{\cos 1} \sin 1<\log _{\sin 1} \cos 1$;
(D) $\log _{\cos 1} \operatorname{tg} 1<\log _{\sin 1} \operatorname{tg} 1<\log _{\sin 1} \cos 1<\log _{\cos 1} \sin 1$.

9. In the Cartesian coordinate system $x O y$, $P$ is a moving point not on the $x$-axis, satisfying the condition: through $P$, two tangent lines can be drawn to the parabola $y^{2}=4 x$, and the line connecting the two points of tangency $l_{P}$ is perpendicular to $P O$. Let the intersection points of the line $l_{P}$ with the line $P O$ and the $x$-axis be $Q$ and $R$, respectively.
(1) Prove that $R$ is a fixed point;
(2) Find the minimum value of $\frac{|P Q|}{|Q R|}$.

8. Six people, all of different weights, are trying to build a human pyramid: that is, they get into the formation
$$
\begin{array}{c}
\text { A } \\
\text { B C } \\
\text { D E F }
\end{array}
$$

We say that someone not in the bottom row is "supported by" each of the two closest people beneath her or him. How many different pyramids are possible, if nobody can be supported by anybody of lower weight?

A mouse is on the below grid:
        \begin{center}
        \begin{asy}
        unitsize(2cm);
        filldraw(circle((0,0),0.07), black);
        filldraw(circle((0,1),0.07), black);
        filldraw(circle((1,0),0.07), black);
        filldraw(circle((0.5,0.5),0.07), black);
        filldraw(circle((1,1),0.07), black);
        draw((0,0)--(1,0));
        draw((0,0)--(0,1));
        draw((1,0)--(1,1));
        draw((0,1)--(1,1));
        draw((0,1)--(0.5,0.5));
        draw((1,0)--(0.5,0.5));
        draw((1,1)--(0.5,0.5));
        draw((0,0)--(0.5,0.5));
        \end{asy}
        \end{center}
         The paths connecting each node are the possible paths the mouse can take to walk from a node to another node. Call a ``turn" the action of a walk from one node to another. Given the mouse starts off on an arbitrary node, what is the expected number of turns it takes for the mouse to return to its original node?

[i]Lightning 4.2[/i]

1. Among the five real numbers below, which one is the smallest?
(A) $\sqrt[209]{2010}$;
(B) $\sqrt[200]{2009}$;
(C) 2010;
(D) $\frac{2010}{2009}$;
(E) $\frac{2009}{2010}$.

B3. Find all pairs of real numbers $x$ and $y$ that solve the system of equations

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{y^{3}+15 x^{2}}{y^{4}-x^{3}}=\frac{y^{2}+15 x}{y^{3}-x^{2}} \\
\frac{1500 y^{3}+4 x^{2}}{9 y^{4}-4}=\frac{1500 y^{2}+4 x}{9 y^{3}-4}
\end{gathered}
$$

## Problems for 2nd Year

Time for solving: 180 minutes. Each problem in set A has exactly one correct answer. In set A, we will award two points for the correct answer, and deduct one point for an incorrect answer. Write the answers for set A in the left table, leave the right table blank. The committee will consider only the answers written in the table for set A.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2024_06_07_716acb2f660115fb0b44g-06.jpg?height=172&width=808&top_left_y=685&top_left_x=634)

Example 1 Let non-negative real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \cdots, a_{n}$ satisfy $a_{1}+$ $a_{2}+\cdots+a_{n}=1$, find
$$
